# inflate — RFC 1951 DEFLATE and RFC 1950 zlib, decompressing.
#
# Three modules, smallest first. `inflate_adler` is RFC 1950 §9's
# checksum and exists because that section is a testable statement on
# its own. `inflate_window` is the 32 KiB sliding window as a circular
# buffer in block RAM, and it owns the one check that decides whether a
# malformed stream reads back somebody else's data: a distance reaching
# behind the start of the stream. `inflate` is everything else — the bit
# reader, the Huffman decoder, the code-length decoder of §3.2.7, the
# copy engine and the framing. `top` names that one.
#
# All three of §3.2.3's block types are decoded. Every malformed input
# is reported on `error_code` and none is hung on, which for a block
# that reads attacker-controlled bytes is a requirement and not a
# feature; `ip/compress/inflate/README.md` §6 and §7 are about that.
#
# There is no compressor here, and no gzip: §9 of the README says what
# each would need and why neither is in this package.
#
# The default window is 262 144 bits, which is sixteen `DP16KD` on an
# ECP5 and more block RAM than a small iCE40 has. `WINDOW_BITS` lowers
# it for a design whose streams were compressed with a smaller window;
# README §5 says what that costs and what it reports.
name inflate
version 1.0.0
license MIT
description "RFC 1951 DEFLATE decompressor with the RFC 1950 zlib wrapper: stored, fixed and dynamic blocks, a block-RAM sliding window, and every malformed stream reported"

top inflate
target ice40
target ecp5

source rtl/inflate_adler.v
source rtl/inflate_window.v
source rtl/inflate.v

param WINDOW_BITS int 15 8..15
param WRAPPER int 1 0..1

port clk in
port rst_n in
port start in
port in_byte in 8
port in_valid in
port in_last in
port in_ready out
port out_byte out 8
port out_valid out
port out_ready in
port done out
port error out
port error_code out 3
port busy out
